const VENV_PY = '.venv/bin/python'
const UV = 'installed tools: uv'
const BASE = 'f51462b'
const common = (wt) => `
Repository: treams-rs (Rust port of the treams T-matrix scattering library; Rust in crates/, Python API in python/treams_rs/). Read ${wt}/AGENTS.md and follow its rules (analytic derivatives only; numerical changes need reference, physical-identity and gradient tests; never loosen tolerances; generated files only via the generator).

Your worktree: ${wt} (branch already checked out, based on commit ${BASE}). Work and commit ONLY there. Never modify treams-rs (another agent's checkout), its .venv, or other worktrees.

Environment:
- Python: always run as PYTHONPATH=${wt}/python ${VENV_PY} ... . The venv's site-packages contains a STALE treams_rs copy; PYTHONPATH makes the worktree sources (and the untracked native build python/treams_rs/_native*.so, which matches the Rust sources at ${BASE}) load instead. Do not delete that .so. Do not pip/uv install into that venv.
- pytest: cd ${wt} && PYTHONPATH=${wt}/python ${VENV_PY} -m pytest <paths> -q -p no:cacheprovider. Run the relevant subsets, not the whole 6k-test suite.
- Lint: ${VENV_PY} -m ruff format <files> and -m ruff check <files>; type check with cd ${wt} && PYTHONPATH=${wt}/python ${VENV_PY} -m pyrefly check --summarize-errors (if it is slow or noisy, at least ensure no new errors in touched files).
- If you change any docstring of a public object, regenerate the docs: cd ${wt} && PYTHONPATH=${wt}/python ${VENV_PY} scripts/generate_docs.py, then confirm --check passes.
- uv 0.12.22 (required by the repo) is at ${UV} if needed.
- Do not edit CHANGELOG.md or CITATION.cff (handled separately).

Commit when done (do not push): git -C ${wt} commit with --author "[name] <[e-mail address]>", a Conventional Commit subject in the repo's plain style (e.g. "fix: accept lattice objects in traced periodic solves"), a short plain-English body, and the trailer line "Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>". Do not mention model names. Keep the diff minimal and in the style of the surrounding code; prefer fixing at the shared point all callers route through.

Return a concise report: what you changed (files), what you ran and the results, anything you could not do.
`

const ITEMS = [
  {
    key: 'periodic-lattice',
    wt: 'treams-rs-fix-periodic-lattice',
    task: `Fix: periodic solves on the automatically selected framework path (Advect, JAX, PyTorch, HIPS Autograd inputs) reject lattice and Bloch-vector forms that the NumPy path accepts.
Verified evidence: python/treams_rs/_framework_tmatrix.py solve_periodic (~line 1188) and _PeriodicInteraction.coupling (~line 134) only call b.array(lattice)/b.array(kpar) and reshape 0-d values, skipping the normalization the NumPy path does in _lattice.geometry_inputs (_lattice.py ~439-453: resolve Lattice alignment/sublattices, take WaveVector components, expand a (dim,) diagonal with np.diag). The framework lattice-sum adapter already repeats that step (python/treams_rs/lattice.py ~970-986). torch._physics_array (torch.py ~170-180) calls torch.tensor(value), which cannot convert a tr.Lattice.
Reproduced failures (radius traced, solve_periodic(...).to_smatrix(PlaneWavePorts.default([0,0])).power(..., side='negative').transmission, vs NumPy central differences):
- tr.Lattice.square(0.9) under PyTorch: TypeError: object of type 'Lattice' has no len() (also p.latticeinteraction.solve(tr.Lattice.square(0.9), [0,0]) and the solve_periodic docstring example in _periodic.py ~255-259 run with a torch radius).
- diagonal list [0.9, 0.95]: all four frameworks raise ValueError 'plane ports require a (2, 2) xy lattice'; NumPy works.
- kpar=tr.WaveVector([0,0]): all four raise 'lattice and Bloch vector must match the requested dimension'; NumPy works.
- traced period passed as diagonal [p, p] (JAX): pullback gradient shape mismatch (expected (2,), received (2,2)) / plane-ports ValueError.
Suggested fix (verifier): move the metadata normalization from lattice._framework_sum into one helper in _lattice.py (e.g. framework_cell(backend, a, kpar, alignment)) that resolves a Lattice via np.asarray(Lattice(a, alignment)), turns a WaveVector into its alignment components, calls backend.array, and expands a (dim,) diagonal as a[:, None] * backend.array(np.eye(dim)) so traced diagonal entries keep their gradient; call it from solve_periodic, _PeriodicInteraction.coupling and _framework_sum (derive alignment like the NumPy path, e.g. periodic_alignment(infer_dimension(a, kpar), spherical)). A deliberately rejected case (3x3 lattice with 2-D kpar, tests/.../test_framework_physics.py ~1106) must stay rejected if it is tested as such.
Add one parametrized test over the Lattice, diagonal-list and WaveVector forms (and a traced diagonal period) for all four engines (skip an engine if its framework is not importable, as the existing tests do), comparing gradients with NumPy central differences. Run the existing framework/periodic/lattice test files too (find them under tests/).`,
  },
  {
    key: 'waves-coords',
    wt: 'treams-rs-fix-waves-coords',
    task: `Two small framework-path fixes.
(1) python/treams_rs/_waves.py plane_wave_angle (~line 672-676): backend = backend_for(theta, phi, pol, k0, material); xp = backend.xp; then xp.sin(theta)*xp.cos(phi) on the raw arguments. torch.sin/cos reject Python floats, so with a torch k0 (or a torch-valued tr.Material medium) and float angles it raises TypeError: sin(): argument 'input' must be Tensor, not float. Fix: when backend is not None, convert theta, phi with backend.array before the trig calls (Backend.array exists in _framework_backend.py ~147). Add a PyTorch test with Python-float angles and a tensor k0 (gradient vs NumPy central difference), and a tensor-Material case; look at how existing framework tests in tests/ are structured and skipped.
(2) Complex coordinates are silently truncated on the framework path: NumPy special.car2sph(complex points) raises TypeError (ufunc not supported), but torch/JAX complex points return the transform of the real part with only a ComplexWarning. The truncation happens in python/treams_rs/diff.py at the shared sink (~line 1978: _native.coordinates_record(np.asarray(points, dtype=np.float64), function)) and for the points argument of vector_coordinates. Fix at that sink: a small helper that raises TypeError for complex input (np.iscomplexobj) before the float64 cast, used for coordinates and the vector_coordinates points. Do not sweep all other float64 casts in diff.py unless one is the same coordinates path. Add tests: complex points raise TypeError via tr.diff.coordinates and via the torch/JAX framework path for car2sph and sph2car; real inputs unchanged.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'diffraction-orders',
    wt: 'treams-rs-fix-diffraction-orders',
